R"VG(
# sunlost8.vg (D-05): the lesson of what the sun was, for the children born in the dark (`dark=3`, eight years and more after the last
# sunrise): a wall painted low for them (the ship reads it); the questions a teacher set down, a primer, a class, the machines' lesson
# and the far future's hum are sunlost9.vg's, over the topics here (`les.topic`, each people its own: the warmth, the light on the crops
# and the herds, the day, the seasons, shadows, the sea moving, the old words) and the way it went (`les.way`). Rules `les.`.
@apart

genre.sunlesson (words=30..185)
  {les.body}

les.body
  (age=prehistoric) {les.pre}
  (age=medieval) {les.med}
  (age=industrial) {les.ind}
  (age=modern) {les.mod}
  (age=futuristic) {les.fut}
  (age=farfuture) {les.ff}

# ---- what the sun was, by topic: a medieval teacher asks each, the later ages tell them ---------------------------------------------

les.topic (people=13)
  (once age=medieval) What was the sun? {les.t.sun}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.sun}
  (once age=medieval) What was a day? {les.t.day}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.day}
  (once age=medieval) What was the morning? {les.t.morning}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.morning}
  (once age=medieval) What was the evening? {les.t.evening}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.evening}
  (once age=medieval) What was noon? {les.t.noon}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.noon}
  (once age=medieval) What was summer? {les.t.summer}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.summer}
  (once seasons=4 age=medieval) What were the seasons? {les.t.seasons}
  (once seasons=4 age!=medieval) {les.t.seasons}
  (once age=medieval) What was warm? {les.t.warm}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.warm}
  (once age=medieval) What colour was the sky? {les.t.sky}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.sky}
  (once desert=0 age=medieval) What was rain? {les.t.rain}
  (once desert=0 age!=medieval) {les.t.rain}
  (once age=medieval) What was the wind? {les.t.wind}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.wind}
  (once age=medieval) What was a shadow? {les.t.shadow}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.shadow}
  (once coast=1 age=medieval) What did the sea do? {les.t.sea}
  (once coast=1 age!=medieval) {les.t.sea}
  (once age=medieval) What did the fields need? {les.t.fields}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.fields}
  (once age=medieval) What did the {herd} do in the sun? {les.t.herd}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.herd}
  (once age=medieval) Was the world always dark? {les.t.colours}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.colours}
  (once age=medieval) What are the old words? {les.t.words}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.words}
  (once age=medieval) How did the old live? {les.t.lived}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.lived}
  (once age=medieval) Could the sun hurt? {les.t.burn}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.burn}
  (once age=medieval) Was there snow then? {les.t.snow}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.snow}
  (once age=medieval) What was the night? {les.t.night}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.night}
  (once age=medieval) Was the sun a god? {les.t.god}
  (once age!=medieval) {les.t.god}
les.t.sun
  The sun was a [fire|light] greater than {mountain}, so far off that it looked no bigger than {vessel.one}, [and it rose every morning|that came up every morning] and [went down every evening|set every evening].
  The sun was a star like [the ones|the stars] we see from the cold door, but so near that its [light|warmth] reached [the whole world|every field] at once.
  It was [a round fire in the sky|the great lamp of the world], [too bright to look at|so bright that nobody could look at it], and it [crossed the sky|went over the sky] once a day.
les.t.day
  The day was the time the sun stood in the sky; [the bells|the old hours] still [count|keep] it, though there is no day now.
  A day was [one turn of the sun|the sun's road over the sky|the sun's crossing], from its [coming up|rising] to its [going down|setting], and the old ones [worked|lived] in it.
  [Every day|Each day] the sun came up and the world [was light|could be seen] to its edges, without a lamp.
les.t.morning
  The sun [came up|rose] over {mountain}, red at first and then [white|gold], and the {bird} [sang|called] for it.
  [In the morning|At first light] the cold of the night went out of the stones, and the {herd} [got up|stood] and turned their faces to the sun.
  The morning was the sun [coming back|returning] after the night, as it always did, [every morning|morning after morning], until it did not.
les.t.evening
  [In the evening|At evening] the sun went down behind {river}, red and [large|swollen], and the sky [turned|went] the colour of fire.
  The evening was the sun [going down|setting], slowly, and the {tree} [laying|throwing] long shadows across the [fields|grass].
  When the sun [had set|was down] the old ones [lit|made] the fires, and it was night, but the night [ended|was short].
les.t.noon
  At noon the sun stood [highest|over our heads], and the stones were [too hot|warm enough] to [sit on|stand on] with bare feet.
  Noon was [the middle of the day|the sun's highest place], when the {herd} [lay in the shade|sought the shade] and nobody worked.
les.t.summer
  Summer was [the long warm time|the warm half of the year], when the sun stood high and [long|late], and the {crop} [ripened|grew tall].
  In summer the days were long and [the nights short|the evenings went on and on]; the children [swam|played|fished] in {river} [until dark|till the stars came out].
les.t.seasons
  The seasons [came round|turned] every year: spring, summer, autumn and winter; [now there is only the cold|we have only the cold now].
  [Every year|Year by year] the world leaned toward the sun and [away again|then away], and so the [summers|warm months] [came and went|came round and went].
les.t.warm
  The stones [at noon|in the afternoon], the water of {river} in summer, a {beast}'s back in the field: all [warm|warmed] by the sun. Now nothing is warm but {warm.ground}.
  [Everything|The whole world] was warm [under the sun|in its light]; you could [lie|sleep] on the ground [outside|under the sky] and [not die of it|wake up warm].
les.t.sky
  (desert=0) The sky was blue by day and [pale|white] at the edges; the stars [hid in it|were hidden] and came out only at night.
  (desert=1) The sky was the colour of [dust and honey|old gold] by day; the stars [hid in it|were hidden] and came out only at night.
  [By day|In the day] there were no stars to see; the sun's light [filled|covered] the whole sky, from {mountain} to the far edge.
les.t.rain
  Rain was water falling [out of the sky|from the clouds], which the sun [drew up|lifted] from the sea and [let fall|gave back] on the fields.
  [The clouds|Clouds] came over the sun and [let go of|dropped] their water, and then the sun came back and [dried|warmed] everything.
les.t.wind
  The wind [came|blew] because the sun warmed the land and not the sea, or the sea and not the land; [now the air does not move|now nothing blows].
  [Winds|The winds] came over {mountain} [every afternoon|in every season], warm or cold, and [moved|bent] the {crop} like water.
les.t.shadow
  A shadow was the dark shape a thing [made|threw] on the ground when it stood in the sun; [the {tree}|every {tree.n}] made long ones in the evening.
  [Everyone|Every one of us] had a shadow then, and it [followed|went everywhere with] them, [short at noon and long in the evening|thin at noon, long at evening].
les.t.sea
  The sea [moved|was never still]: it came up the shore and went back [twice a day|again], and it shone [so that you could not look at it|like metal]; [now it is stone|it is stone now].
  The water [ran|moved] then, under the wind; the {fish} [rose|came up] to the light, and the boats went out on it[, where now it is ice to the bottom|; now it is ice from shore to shore].
les.t.fields
  The fields [needed|drank] the sun as they [needed|drank] the rain; the {crop} [turned to it|followed it] and [grew|ripened] gold.
  Nothing grew without the sun, not the {crop}, not the grass, not the {tree}; [what we eat now grows|our {dark.food} grow] [by the warm stones|only by {warm.ground}].
les.t.herd
  The {herd} [lay|stood] in the sun to be warm, and [walked|grazed] on the hills [all day|from morning to evening].
  [In the sun|Under the sun] the {herd} grew fat on the grass of the hills; [now the last of them|the few we kept] live below with us.
les.t.colours
  The world was [green and gold|full of colours] by day: the {crop}, the {tree}, the water of {river}, [the red evenings|the blue noons][; now it is the colour of the lamps|, where now there is only lamplight].
  (age=medieval) No: by day you could see [every colour|the colours of everything], and [from far off|a long way off], without a lamp; [the dark came only at night|only the night was dark].
  (age!=medieval) By day you could see [every colour|the colours of everything], and [from far off|a long way off], without a lamp; [the dark came only at night|only the night was dark].
les.t.words
  [Dawn|Daylight], [noon|sunset] and [summer|morning] were words for the sun's doings; we keep them, and [they mean nothing now|nobody alive has seen what they mean].
  [The old ones' words|The words of the old songs] "a bright day" and "the sun on your face" [meant|were about] [the sun's light|real warmth], not a lamp.
les.t.lived
  The old ones [worked|lived] by the sun: they [rose|got up] with it, worked in its light, and [slept|lay down] when it went down.
  They kept {festival} [when the sun stood highest|at midsummer], and [built their doors|turned their houses] to face [the morning|the sunrise].
les.t.burn
  (age=medieval) Yes: [stay out too long|sleep in the open at noon] and it [burnt|reddened] your skin; it could blind a [child|careless one] who looked at it.
  (age!=medieval) The sun could [burn|redden] your skin if you stayed out too long, and it could blind a [child|careless one] who looked at it.
  [Too much sun|A long day in the fields] could [burn|sicken] you; the old ones [wore hats|kept to the shade] at noon.
les.t.snow
  (age=medieval) Only in [winter|the cold months]; when the sun [came back|grew strong] the snow [melted|ran off] into {river}, every year.
  (age!=medieval) There was snow only in [winter|the cold months]; when the sun [came back|grew strong] it [melted|ran off] into {river}, every year.
  The snow [came|fell] in winter and [went|melted] in spring; it lay on {mountain} [all year|the year round], and nowhere else.
les.t.night
  The night was [short|only half the day], [between two days|the sun's absence], and the stars [came out|were seen] only then; now it does not end.
  [At night|In the night] the sun was on the other side of the world, and [everyone|the whole people] knew it would [come back in the morning|rise again].
les.t.god
  (faith=sky) The old priests said it was {god}'s eye, [and that it closed|and that he turned it away], [because of us|for our wrongs].
  (faith!=sky) [Some said|The old ones said] it was {god}'s fire; [the teacher says|we say] only that it was [a fire, and ours|the greatest fire there was|a fire like ours, only greater].

# why it went (`les.way`: the way as a clause, each of its parts its own choice), why they live below, whether it comes back
les.way
  (sunway=wanderer) [the wanderer|the great wanderer|the giant of our sky] [came nearer|drew closer|grew brighter] [every year|year after year|with every winter], [pulled the world out of its road|drew our road out long|dragged us away from the sun], and [the year stretched|the summers came later and shorter|the sun grew small]
  (sunway=intruder) [a new star|a stranger star|a second light] [came out of the dark|grew in the sky|crept nearer], brighter [every year|each generation], and [took the sun's hold on us with it when it went|when it passed the sun let us go|when it left it took us with it]
  (sunway=dark) [something that gave no light|a thing nobody could see|a darkness that did not shine] [passed near|went by close|came near], [the stars bent round it|the stars slid aside round it], and [the year came apart|our road round the sun broke|the world was thrown out]
  (sunway=dance) [the two suns|our sun and its sister|the sun and the second sun] [danced further apart|drew apart|pulled the year longer] [every year|for generations], [until ours let us go|until the one we belonged to let go|and one year did not give it back]
les.why
  Why did it go? Because {les.way}.
  Why did it go? The old ones saw it coming: {les.way}.
  [Why did the sun go?|Who took the sun?] Nobody took it; {les.way}.
les.below
  [Why do we live below?|Why are we under the ground?] Because the warmth is only in the ground now, at {warm.ground}; [so we came down, and we stayed|so we came down to it].
  [Why do we eat|Why must we eat] {dark.food}? Because nothing grows without the sun but what grows by {warm.ground}, [in the dark|in the warm dark].
  Will it come back? No[, and the teacher will not say it will|; do not let anyone tell you it will|, never]. [It is still there|It has not gone out], the brightest of the stars, [from the cold door|at the top of the shaft|over the high vents].

# ---- the old time: a wall painted low, at a child's height ---------------------------------------------------------------------

les.pre
  {les.pre.where} {les.pre.sun} {les.pre.under} {les.pre.ship}
  {les.pre.where} {les.pre.sun} {les.pre.then} {les.pre.hands}
  {les.pre.where} {les.pre.sun} {les.pre.count} {les.pre.ship}
  {les.pre.where} {les.pre.sun} {les.pre.under} {les.pre.then} {les.pre.hands}
les.pre.where (formula)
  (medium=painted) Painted low on the wall of {hall.below}, at a child's height.
  (medium=painted) On the wall by {warm.ground}, where the children slept, a picture.
  (medium=painted) Low on the rock, under the soot of the cooking fires, a picture made for the small ones.
  (medium=stone) Cut into a flat stone by {warm.ground}, the lines rubbed smooth by many small hands.
  (medium=stone) Pecked into a stone that was carried down into {hall.below} and set where the children sat.
  (medium=bone) Cut along a {pre.bone}, small, and worn by fingers.
  (medium=bone) Scratched on a {pre.bone} that was passed from hand to hand round the fire.
les.pre.sun
  A [great|large] round sign in {colour.holy}, [larger than any figure near it|bigger than the {herd}], with lines running out of it [all round|on every side].
  A circle of {colour.holy} with [strokes|lines] coming out of it on every side, like the hair of a {beast}.
  A ring of [hand prints|small hands] round an empty circle, the circle left the colour of the rock.
  A round fire [drawn high up|set at the top], with nothing holding it, [bigger than the {herd} under it|over everything else].
les.pre.under
  Under it the {herd} stand in a row, each with a dark shape [lying on the ground beside it|at its feet]; their shadows, the ship thinks, which the children never had.
  Under it the {crop} stand in lines, and the figures among them hold their arms open, their faces turned [up|to it].
  Under it {river} runs in wavy lines, [broken with small marks|with short strokes on it]: the light on the water.
  Under it a figure lies on its back [in the open|on the grass], with nothing over it but the round sign.

# the medieval wall's words (sunlost9.vg's `les.med`)
les.med.words
  "This was the sun. It warmed the world and made the day. It went before your fathers were born."
  "Remember the sun, that gave the {crop} and the day; it is a star now, and the brightest of them."
  "Here the children of {city} learn what the sun was, that the old ones saw, and that their children will not."
  "Under the sun the {herd} grazed and the {crop} grew. Under the ground we wait."
les.med.after
  The children's names are scratched round it, [many of them|dozens of them], [the oldest worn away|some very old].
  The rays have been [gilded|picked out in {colour.holy}], and the colour rubbed away by hands.
  Someone has added, smaller, one of the {herd} under the sun.
)VG"
